===Etymology===
From {{compound|en|ghost|writer}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Flame, not lame-ghostwriter.wav|a=US}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A professional [[writer]] who is paid to write material that is officially [[credit]]ed to another person; one who writes on [[behalf]] of someone else, often for a [[celebrity]].
#: {{ux|en|Smith was listed as the author of his autobiography, but a '''ghostwriter''' did most of the work.}}
#* {{RQ:Time|title=How Celebrity Memoirs Got So Good|author=Cady Lang|date=April 13 2021
|passage='''Ghostwriters''', typically the faceless forces behind many books by famous non-writers, are now becoming more visible, with celebrities openly teaming up with high-profile professionals.
|url=https://time.com/5954152/celebrity-memoirs/|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20210428083032/https://time.com/5954152/celebrity-memoirs/}}
#* {{RQ:NYT|author=w:Michelle Goldberg|title=JD Vance Just Blurbed a Book Arguing That Progressives Are Subhuman|date=2024-08-05|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2024/08/05/opinion/jd-vance-fascism-unhumans.html|passage=But “Unhumans,” an anti-democratic screed that Posobiec co-wrote with the professional '''ghostwriter''' Joshua Lisec, comes with endorsements from some of the most influential people in Republican politics, including, most significantly, vice-presidential candidate JD Vance.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20240824071159/https://www.nytimes.com/2024/08/05/opinion/jd-vance-fascism-unhumans.html}}

